<div class="scene-image"><<locationImage "day1f_waking" "Waking">></div>

<h2>Recovery Room   Waking</h2>

<p>Something is wrong.</p>

<p>I know this before I open my eyes. I know it the way you know you're falling before you hit the ground   a full-body wrongness, a vibration in every nerve that says <em>this is not what you were.</em> My body is lying on a surface that should be familiar   a bed, linens, a pillow   but the way I'm lying on it is different. My weight distributes differently. My hips are pressing into the mattress at an angle I don't recognise. My chest has weight on it   small, warm, settling against my ribcage with a foreign gravity.</p>

<p>I open my eyes.</p>

<p>Ceiling. Not mine. Not the cracked stone of my chambers with the smoke-stain halo and the mapped constellation of imperfections I've known since I was twelve. This ceiling is white   the luminous white stone of the Moonspire Tower, faintly glowing even in daylight. Smooth. Unmarked. The ceiling of a room that belongs to no one and everyone who has ever needed it.</p>

<p>Sunlight through narrow windows. The medicinal smell of the Silverveil   dried herbs, beeswax, that electric-ozone undertone of ambient magic. My mouth tastes like copper and flowers. The potion. I remember the potion. I remember the taste of it   moonlight and blood   and then the pain, the grinding restructuring of bone and tissue and the sound of my own scream changing pitch </p>

<p>My hair.</p>

<p>It's everywhere. Dark waves spread across the pillow, across my shoulders, across my face. I didn't have this much hair. I had hair that fell over my forehead and needed pushing back and wouldn't stay behind my ears. This is different   longer, thicker, heavier, cascading over everything with the undiscriminating enthusiasm of hair that doesn't know it wasn't here yesterday.</p>

<p>I push it away. My hand is wrong.</p>

<p>I stare at it. The fingers are slender where they were thick, the knuckles smooth where they were knobbed and scarred. The palm is narrower. The nail beds are wider, the nails themselves longer   clean, oval, pink. No calluses. Six years of sword work, six years of gripping a practice blade until the leather wrapping wore smooth, and the calluses are gone. The magic took them. The magic took everything.</p>

<p>I make a fist. The fist is smaller. The tendons that stand up along the back of the hand are finer, more delicate. I can see the veins   blue, close to the surface, the skin thinner and almost translucent. These are hands for threading needles, for playing harps, for resting on the arm of a throne. I cannot imagine them gripping a sword.</p>

<p>I sit up.</p>

<p>The motion is wrong. My core engages differently   the abdominal muscles shorter, the centre of gravity lower, and there is weight on my chest that shifts when I rise, pulling forward, settling against my ribcage with a soft, warm, undeniable presence. I look down.</p>

<p>Breasts.</p>

<p>They're small. Barely more than swelling   high on my chest, firm, the kind of breast that belongs to a young woman whose body is still building itself. But they're unmistakably breasts. The nightgown someone dressed me in   white linen, thin   drapes over them, and I can see the shape through the fabric: two small mounds, the nipples pressing against the linen, hard from the cold air and from the fact that everything is unbearably sensitive and my body is registering stimuli through nerve endings that didn't exist twenty-four hours ago.</p>

<p>I throw the blankets off. The motion sends ripples through the mattress and those ripples travel through the breasts and the breasts move   a small, barely perceptible bounce that I feel in the tissue and the skin and the nipples and every other square inch of new flesh that has apparently decided to be responsive to gravity, vibration, and the basic mechanics of existing in space.</p>

<p>I swing my legs over the side of the bed. My legs. They're shorter   the distance to the floor is different, my feet dangle where they used to reach. The thighs are thicker, rounder, the calves curved in a way I associate with women I've watched walk across ballrooms but never with my own body crossing a room. The skin is smooth. No hair. None. Just skin, pale and soft and absurdly sensitive, registering the temperature differential between the warm bed and the cool air with a level of detail that seems excessive and unnecessary.</p>

<p>My feet. They're smaller. They look like they belong to someone else   narrow, arched, the toes shorter. I flex them. They respond. They're mine. Everything in this body responds to my commands. It's just that the body issuing the commands and the body executing them are no longer recognisable to each other.</p>

<p>I stand.</p>

<p>I'm short. The room hasn't changed. The window I looked through yesterday   the narrow one that framed the bridge and the palace beyond   now requires me to stretch. The table beside the bed is higher. The mirror across the room is hung at a height that no longer centres on my face. The world has grown, or I have shrunk, and the disorientation is absolute.</p>

<p>Between my legs   nothing.</p>

<p>Not nothing. But the absence of the familiar, which is worse than nothing because it occupies the same space. The familiar weight   the comfortable, unremarkable hang of cock and balls against my thigh, the warmth, the way they shifted when I walked and settled when I sat   is gone. Replaced by a smooth absence, a soft mound, folds I can feel pressing against each other with every motion, a warmth that comes from inside rather than outside. An architecture I don't have the vocabulary for yet.</p>

<p class="internal-thought"><em>I chose this. Two nights ago, I said the word "Complete" out loud, in my mother's arms, and meant it. I held myself in the dark and let go on purpose. This absence isn't something that was done to me. It's something I did. The form was mine to give, and I gave it.</em></p>

<p class="internal-thought"><em>Knowing that doesn't make the grief smaller. It just means the grief is mine too.</em></p>

<p>I make it three steps before my knees give.</p>

<p>Not from weakness. The muscles work   they're different muscles, arranged differently, firing on slightly adjusted timelines, but they work. What fails is something deeper. The part of the brain that has spent twenty years building a model of the body it controls, calibrating every movement to a specific height, a specific weight, a specific distribution of mass   that model has been invalidated overnight, and the discrepancy between expectation and reality is enough to drop me.</p>

<p>I sit on the cold stone floor. The stone is colder than it should be   thinner skin, more nerve endings, less insulation. I put my head between my knees. My knees press together differently. My hips are wider. My thighs meet in a way they never did   higher, warmer, the inner surfaces touching along a greater length. I can feel the vulva between them. Soft. Warm. Present with an insistence that borders on aggressive.</p>

<p>I don't cry. The shock is too complete for tears. Tears require processing, and processing requires the engagement of emotional faculties that are currently standing at the edge of a cliff looking down and refusing to jump.</p>

<p>My hands are in my lap. Someone else's hands in someone else's lap. These aren't my hands. This isn't my </p>

<p>I touch my face. The jaw is soft. The sharp angles I catalogued three days ago, standing before the mirror in my chambers   gone. Rounded, feminine, a jawline that curves where it used to cut. No stubble. The skin is smooth in a way that goes beyond shaving   there is no stubble, no grain, no subdermal roughness. The follicles are gone. My cheekbones are higher. My lips are fuller   I can feel them with my fingertips, the lower lip plumper, the bow of the upper lip more pronounced. My nose is smaller, narrower. My brow is smooth   the heavy ridge that sat above my eyes like a shelf is gone, the forehead rounded and open.</p>

<p>I run my tongue over my teeth. The same. The teeth are the same. The same slight overlap of the lower front teeth that I've had since childhood. The same chip on the upper right canine from biting into an olive pit at twelve.</p>

<p>For some reason, this makes it worse. The teeth are the same. Everything else is different, rewritten, replaced, but the teeth   the most interior, most hidden part of the face   are unchanged. As if the magic decided the teeth were irrelevant, or the teeth decided they weren't participating, or some fundamental architectural element of who I am lives in the enamel and refused to be overwritten.</p>

<p>I am sitting on a cold floor in a tower room in a body I don't recognise, and the only thing connecting me to the person I was yesterday is a chipped canine and a pair of grey-blue eyes.</p>

